1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,895
A blade of grass.

2
00:00:01,895 --> 00:00:03,059
A mango leaf.

3
00:00:03,059 --> 00:00:04,504
A grain of maize.

4
00:00:04,904 --> 00:00:07,155
Hold the two leaves up to the light.

5
00:00:07,155 --> 00:00:08,934
The grass has straight lines.

6
00:00:08,934 --> 00:00:10,588
The mango leaf has a net.

7
00:00:11,038 --> 00:00:13,361
Diya noticed this in the college garden.

8
00:00:13,361 --> 00:00:17,643
She wants to be a doctor, and she
asked: why lines in one, and a net in

9
00:00:17,643 --> 00:00:18,543
the other?

10
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,086
That one clue splits all flowering
plants into just two big groups.

11
00:00:23,586 --> 00:00:28,079
By the end, you will tell the two groups
apart from a seed, a root, a leaf,

12
00:00:28,079 --> 00:00:29,413
a stem and a flower.

13
00:00:30,063 --> 00:00:32,152
First, what is an angiosperm?

14
00:00:32,502 --> 00:00:34,531
Angio means a closed vessel.

15
00:00:34,531 --> 00:00:35,864
Sperm means seed.

16
00:00:35,864 --> 00:00:39,497
In angiosperms, the seeds
are closed inside a fruit.

17
00:00:39,947 --> 00:00:42,876
That is why we call them
the flowering plants.

18
00:00:42,876 --> 00:00:45,161
Their sex organs sit inside flowers.

19
00:00:45,611 --> 00:00:50,689
They range from Wolffia, a floating plant
almost too small to see, to Eucalyptus

20
00:00:50,689 --> 00:00:53,044
trees over one hundred metres tall.

21
00:00:53,694 --> 00:00:56,617
Let's zoom into one flower,
one level at a time.

22
00:00:56,968 --> 00:00:59,236
A flower has stamens and a pistil.

23
00:00:59,236 --> 00:01:02,136
The anther of each stamen
makes pollen grains.

24
00:01:02,636 --> 00:01:03,536
Zoom in.

25
00:01:03,417 --> 00:01:06,446
The swollen base of the
pistil is the ovary.

26
00:01:06,446 --> 00:01:07,955
It holds the ovules.

27
00:01:08,455 --> 00:01:09,997
Zoom into one ovule.

28
00:01:09,997 --> 00:01:11,962
Inside sits the embryo sac.

29
00:01:11,962 --> 00:01:16,378
It is the female gametophyte, the
tiny plant that makes the egg.

30
00:01:16,878 --> 00:01:19,105
The embryo sac has seven cells.

31
00:01:19,105 --> 00:01:23,970
One egg cell, two synergids, three
antipodal cells, and one large central

32
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:25,928
cell with two polar nuclei.

33
00:01:26,578 --> 00:01:29,996
Now watch a process that
only flowering plants do.

34
00:01:29,996 --> 00:01:31,471
Double fertilisation.

35
00:01:31,821 --> 00:01:32,721
Step one.

36
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:37,298
A pollen grain lands on the stigma,
and grows a pollen tube down to the

37
00:01:37,298 --> 00:01:38,198
ovule.

38
00:01:38,664 --> 00:01:39,564
Step two.

39
00:01:39,482 --> 00:01:43,024
The tube carries two male
gametes into the embryo sac.

40
00:01:43,774 --> 00:01:44,731
Step three.

41
00:01:44,731 --> 00:01:47,247
One male gamete fuses with the egg.

42
00:01:47,247 --> 00:01:48,527
This is syngamy.

43
00:01:48,527 --> 00:01:50,076
It makes the zygote.

44
00:01:50,826 --> 00:01:51,726
Step four.

45
00:01:51,715 --> 00:01:55,341
The other male gamete fuses
with the two polar nuclei.

46
00:01:55,341 --> 00:01:58,854
Three nuclei join, so this
is called triple fusion.

47
00:01:58,854 --> 00:02:01,469
It makes the primary endosperm nucleus.

48
00:02:02,219 --> 00:02:03,119
Step five.

49
00:02:03,107 --> 00:02:08,089
The zygote grows into the embryo, and
the endosperm becomes its food store.

50
00:02:08,089 --> 00:02:10,028
The ovule becomes the seed.

51
00:02:10,028 --> 00:02:12,019
The ovary becomes the fruit.

52
00:02:12,769 --> 00:02:14,269
Here is the big split.

53
00:02:14,269 --> 00:02:16,468
Look at the embryo inside the seed.

54
00:02:16,818 --> 00:02:19,299
It carries cotyledons, the seed leaves.

55
00:02:19,299 --> 00:02:22,460
They are the first leaves,
packed inside the seed.

56
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:26,249
Monocots have one cotyledon,
like maize and wheat.

57
00:02:26,249 --> 00:02:28,776
Dicots have two, like pea and gram.

58
00:02:29,426 --> 00:02:30,326
Roots.

59
00:02:30,046 --> 00:02:32,351
A dicot keeps one main tap root.

60
00:02:32,351 --> 00:02:37,164
In a monocot, the first root dies
early, and many thin fibrous roots take

61
00:02:37,164 --> 00:02:38,779
over, like in wheat.

62
00:02:39,529 --> 00:02:40,429
Leaves.

63
00:02:40,210 --> 00:02:44,070
Monocot leaves have parallel
veins, like the grass blade.

64
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:48,683
Dicot leaves have a net of veins,
called reticulate venation, like the

65
00:02:48,683 --> 00:02:49,632
mango leaf.

66
00:02:50,382 --> 00:02:51,282
Stems.

67
00:02:50,973 --> 00:02:55,752
Cut a monocot stem, and the vascular
bundles, the pipes for water and food,

68
00:02:55,752 --> 00:02:56,787
are scattered.

69
00:02:56,787 --> 00:02:59,389
In a dicot stem, they sit in a ring.

70
00:03:00,139 --> 00:03:01,039
Flowers.

71
00:03:00,963 --> 00:03:04,290
Monocot flower parts
usually come in threes.

72
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:08,104
Dicot flower parts usually
come in fours or fives.

73
00:03:08,854 --> 00:03:09,776
Your turn.

74
00:03:09,776 --> 00:03:13,887
A plant has a tap root, and
leaves with a net of veins.

75
00:03:13,887 --> 00:03:15,243
Monocot or dicot?

76
00:03:15,243 --> 00:03:16,599
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:19,799 --> 00:03:20,699
Dicot!

78
00:03:20,439 --> 00:03:24,616
A tap root and a net of veins
both point to two cotyledons.

79
00:03:25,266 --> 00:03:26,330
Remember this.

80
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:30,564
One cotyledon or two: that one
count splits the flowering plants.

81
00:03:31,164 --> 00:03:32,765
Now, a worked example.

82
00:03:32,765 --> 00:03:35,199
Let's sort six plants, one clue each.

83
00:03:35,549 --> 00:03:37,529
Wheat has fibrous roots.

84
00:03:37,529 --> 00:03:38,429
Monocot.

85
00:03:38,811 --> 00:03:40,716
Mango has a net of veins.

86
00:03:40,716 --> 00:03:41,616
Dicot.

87
00:03:41,791 --> 00:03:45,070
Each pea seed opens into two cotyledons.

88
00:03:45,070 --> 00:03:45,970
Dicot.

89
00:03:46,268 --> 00:03:49,036
A maize grain holds one cotyledon.

90
00:03:49,036 --> 00:03:49,936
Monocot.

91
00:03:50,337 --> 00:03:52,608
Mustard flowers have four petals.

92
00:03:52,608 --> 00:03:53,508
Dicot.

93
00:03:53,688 --> 00:03:57,800
And grass, like the blade
Diya held, has parallel veins.

94
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:58,700
Monocot.

95
00:03:59,208 --> 00:04:00,674
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:01,024 --> 00:04:04,362
Many students think a dicot
is a plant with two seeds.

97
00:04:04,762 --> 00:04:07,920
But di counts the
cotyledons, the seed leaves.

98
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:10,251
One pea seed holds two cotyledons.

99
00:04:10,251 --> 00:04:12,430
One maize grain holds just one.

100
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:14,495
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:14,495 --> 00:04:18,798
As feature matching: which set of
features belongs to a monocot, or to

102
00:04:18,798 --> 00:04:19,698
a dicot.

103
00:04:20,130 --> 00:04:21,384
Exam level now.

104
00:04:21,384 --> 00:04:23,128
Read the four features.

105
00:04:23,128 --> 00:04:25,424
Which ones belong to a monocot?

106
00:04:25,424 --> 00:04:26,732
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:32,132 --> 00:04:33,422
The answer is A.

108
00:04:33,422 --> 00:04:35,522
Features one, three and four.

109
00:04:36,022 --> 00:04:37,161
Two is wrong.

110
00:04:37,161 --> 00:04:39,334
A tap root belongs to dicots.

111
00:04:39,334 --> 00:04:41,328
Monocots have fibrous roots.

112
00:04:41,828 --> 00:04:46,893
Parallel veins, scattered vascular
bundles and flower parts in threes are

113
00:04:46,893 --> 00:04:48,404
all monocot marks.

114
00:04:49,004 --> 00:04:50,467
Here is your recap.

115
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:51,972
Screenshot this card.

116
00:04:55,172 --> 00:04:58,170
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:04:58,570 --> 00:04:59,470
One.

118
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:01,945
Name the seven cells of the embryo sac.

119
00:05:02,845 --> 00:05:03,745
Two.

120
00:05:03,296 --> 00:05:06,071
What is triple fusion,
and what does it make?

121
00:05:06,971 --> 00:05:07,871
Three.

122
00:05:07,604 --> 00:05:12,267
Give two ways to tell a monocot
from a dicot without opening a seed.

123
00:05:13,367 --> 00:05:17,191
Next time: a moss lives
two lives that take turns.

124
00:05:17,191 --> 00:05:18,091
How?

125
00:05:17,757 --> 00:05:20,828
We find out in alternation of generations.
